    "construction_type": {
      "group": "envelope",
      "binding": "specified",
      "confidence": "high",
      "variants": [
        {
          "id": "solid-brick-masonry",
          "status": "canonical",
          "note": "description.long: brick from an early date, 'a hard, small, red-orange brick laid in thick lime mortar' -- the primary material across the whole region, west especially: 'West of the Scheldt the Flemish wall is solid brick and the frame disappears entirely' (own distinguished_from note against german-fachwerk)."
        },
        {
          "id": "braced-timber-frame",
          "status": "permitted",
          "note": "CASCADE-PROVENANCE PARTIAL FIX. The inherited germanic-vernacular binding made timber frame the sole canonical form, which overstates it: this node's own text scopes timber framing to 'the eastern (Limburg, Kempen, Maasland) half of the region,' 'structurally continuous with Rhenish Fachwerk,' with solid brick as the western and more general case. Hard constraint c01 is explicit both are legitimate but ARE ALTERNATIVES, NOT LAYERS: 'a vakwerk elevation must stand on a fired-brick or stone plinth... and must not be applied decoratively over a masonry wall.'"
        }
      ],
      "rule": "Solid brick is the general and western case; timber frame with brick nogging (vakwerk) survives only in the eastern Limburg/Kempen/Maasland belt, on its own fired-brick or stone plinth at least 400 mm above grade (hard constraint c01) -- the two are alternative systems, never one applied decoratively over the other.",
      "sources": [
        "flemish-vernacular.json description.long, distinguished_from, constraints c01, proportion_packs (timber-bay note)"
      ],
      "status": "drafted"
    },
